{"number": 29, "text": "                       ac'celerate            and      ac   cele'ration\n                       Suffixes          and      word       stress       (2)\n\n                    1. In words  with the following suffixes, main stress is usually on the syllable immediately before the\n                       suffix: -ial, -ic, -ion, -ive, and -ity. For example:\n                              ‘editor — edi'torial                     ‘atmosphere  — ,atmos'pheric\n                              ac'celerate — ac,cele'ration             ‘instinct — in'stinctive\n                                        — ,gene'rosity                 di'verse — di'versity\n                 A     Note: In words ending -ative, stress is usually on the same syllable as in the root word. For example:\n                             in'vestigate in'vestigative          ‘speculate  ‘speculative\n\n                       Many   words  with these suffixes can have stress shift (see Unit 10). For example:\n                              He  faces proseCUtion.   but:  He’s a PROSecution   WITness.\n\n                       When   a word  ends with one  of the consonants t or s and the suffix -ion, this is how they are\n                       pronounced:\n                       -tion is pronounced   /tfan/ after the letter s: suggestion, digestion\n                                             /fan/ after other letters: education, adoption\n                       -sion is              /fan/ after a consonant: extension, comprehension\n                                             /3an/ after a vowel: decision, persuasion\n                       -ssion is pronounced  /fan/: admission, expression\n\n                  AS2  Tn nouns  and adjectives ending with the suffixes -ant, -ent, -ance, or -ence, stress placement\n                       depends  on the spelling of the syllable before the suffix (the pre-suffix syllable).\n                       e If the pre-suffix syllable ends with a single vowel letter (V) or a single vowel letter plus a\n                         single consonant  letter (VC), stress usually goes on the syllable before the pre-suffix syllable\n                         if there is one:\n                              ‘ignorant (VC)              ‘variant (V)                   ‘fraudulent (VC)\n                              con'tinuance  (V)                     (VC)                 ‘ambience  (V)\n                       e If the pre-suffix syllable has any other spelling, then stress is usually on the pre-suffix\n                         syllable itself:\n                              ap'pearance  (VVC)          icorre'spondent (VCC)          con'vergence  (VCC)\n                       e If the pre-suffix syllable ends with the letter i and the root word ends with the letter y in a\n                         stressed syllable, the stress is usually on the pre-suffix syllable:\n                              com'ply  — com'pliance      re'ly — re'liant\n\n                       Some  of these words  ending with  the suffixes -ant, -ent, -ance or -ence have a different stress\n                       placement  from  the root:\n                              ig'nore — ‘ignorant         re'fer —\n\n                       while others have  the same stress placement:\n                              con'tinue — con'tinuance            — ap'pearance\n                       Notice  that the suffix -ment doesn’t usually change the stress pattern in the root:\n                              a'gree — a'greement                 — 'government\n\n                       although  a common   exception  is:           — ad'vertisement\n\n                       You  can find more  practice of words with  suffixes in Section E3.\n\n\n              30          English Pronunciation in Use (Advanced)", "char_count": 3761, "mean_conf": 90.35, "word_count": 387, "words": [{"text": "ac'celerate", "x0": 437, "y0": 108, "x1": 831, "y1": 171, "conf": 90, "bold": false